 What is Path Traversal?

Path Traversal is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to access restricted files on a server by manipulating file path inputs.

It typically occurs when user input is directly used in file system operations without proper validation.

Concept Flow:
Application expects a safe file path
Attacker injects traversal sequences (../)
Server resolves path outside intended directory
Sensitive files are exposed

 Basic Example
GET /download?file=report.pdf
 Exploit:
GET /download?file=../../../../etc/passwd

If vulnerable → server returns system file contents

 Impact
Access to sensitive system files (/etc/passwd)
Exposure of application source code
Credential leakage
Remote code execution (in chained attacks)

Common Payloads & Techniques
1. Basic Traversal Sequences
../
../../
../../../

Used to move up directories.

2. Absolute Path Access
/etc/passwd
C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts

3. URL Encoded Payloads
../ → %2e%2e%2f

Example:

GET /file?name=%2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2fetc/passwd

4. Double Encoding (Filter Bypass)
../ → %252e%252e%252f

Bypasses weak filters that decode only once.

5. Mixed Encoding
..%2f
%2e./

6. Null Byte Injection (Legacy)
file=../../etc/passwd%00.png

Tricks backend into ignoring appended extensions.

7. Path Traversal with Different Separators
Linux:
/
Windows:
\

Example:

..\\..\\Windows\\system.ini

Interesting Target Files
LINUX
/etc/passwd - all registered users that have access to the system
/etc/shadow - information about the system users password
/var/www/html/config.php

WINDOWS
C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts
C:\boot.ini

Common Vulnerable Code Patterns
❌ Node.js Example
app.get("/file", (req, res) => {
  const filePath = "uploads/" + req.query.name;
  res.sendFile(filePath);
});

 No validation → vulnerable

❌ PHP Example
include($_GET['page']);
❌ Python Example
open("files/" + filename)

 Prevention Techniques
✅ 1. Use Whitelisting

Allow only specific filenames:

const allowedFiles = ["report.pdf", "data.csv"];
✅ 2. Normalize & Validate Paths

Resolve path safely:

const path = require("path");
const safePath = path.normalize(userInput);
✅ 3. Restrict Base Directory

Ensure file stays inside allowed directory:

if (!safePath.startsWith(baseDir)) {
  return res.status(403).send("Access denied");
}
✅ 4. Avoid Direct File Access from User Input

Use mapping:

?id=1 → report.pdf
✅ 5. Use Secure APIs

Framework-level protections (e.g., safe file handlers)

My Key Takeaway

Path Traversal is not about clever payloads.
It’s about the system blindly trusting file paths.

If user input touches the filesystem unchecked, the attacker is already halfway inside.
